  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": "7045bfdc-b480-4dda-8af3-6aaf58fd604a",
      "name": "Sticky Note - Overview",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        -1072,
        -400
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "width": 1088,
        "height": 940,
        "content": "## Event-Driven Architecture (Advanced)\n\nA reusable, general-purpose event bus built in n8n. Any producer (webhook, cron, another workflow) publishes an *event envelope* — `{eventType, eventId, source, timestamp, payload}` — into a single **Event Ingress** entry point. From there:\n\n1. **Validation & enrichment** — envelope is schema-checked and stamped with routing metadata\n2. **Event Store (append-only log)** — every event is persisted before anything else happens, so nothing is ever routed without first being durably recorded (event sourcing pattern)\n3. **Router (Switch)** — routes by `eventType` to one or more subscriber branches, fan-out style; unmatched types fall through to a catch-all\n4. **Subscribers** — independent branches (order service, notification service, analytics service...) each with their own try/fail handling — one subscriber failing never blocks another\n5. **Retry with backoff** — a failed subscriber delivery is retried a bounded number of times with increasing delay\n6. **Dead-Letter Queue** — deliveries that exhaust retries are written to a DLQ table/queue and raise a Slack alert, instead of silently vanishing\n7. **Ack** — successful delivery is recorded back on the event store row\n\nThis is the classic **pub/sub + event sourcing + DLQ** trio, implemented with n8n-native building blocks so it runs anywhere n8n runs, with no external broker required (swap the Postgres-style HTTP nodes for Kafka/RabbitMQ/SQS via their REST APIs if you outgrow this).\n\n### Setup\n1. Import into n8n\n2. Set - Event Bus Config: event store endpoint, DLQ endpoint, retry policy, Slack channel, subscriber endpoints\n3. Point each 'Subscriber -' HTTP node at your real service (or replace with a Function/DB node for in-n8n logic)\n4. Wire your real producers (webhooks, other workflows via Execute Workflow, schedule triggers) into 'Event Ingress - Webhook' or trigger this as a sub-workflow with 'Event Ingress - Execute Workflow Trigger'\n5. Activate\n\n### Customize\n- Add a subscriber: duplicate a 'Subscriber -' branch off the Switch node, add its eventType case\n- Change fan-out to fan-in: replace Switch with a Merge if you want every event to hit every subscriber unconditionally\n- Swap the event store / DLQ HTTP nodes for a native Postgres/Airtable node if you prefer"
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "aeeaceba-3f5f-4f22-9c29-aae1c6e37390",
      "name": "Sticky Note - Stage 1",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        272,
        -432
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 6,
        "width": 1072,
        "height": 940,
        "content": "## 1. Event Ingress, Validation & Durable Log\n\nTwo entry points feed the same pipeline:\n- **Event Ingress - Webhook**: external producers POST an event envelope\n- **Event Ingress - Execute Workflow Trigger**: other n8n workflows publish events by calling this workflow directly (in-process pub/sub, no HTTP hop)\n\nSet - Event Bus Config centralizes the event store URL, DLQ URL, retry policy (max attempts, base delay), subscriber endpoints, and Slack channel.\n\nCode 1 - Validate & Enrich Envelope checks the envelope has eventType, eventId (generates one if missing), source, timestamp, and payload; rejects malformed envelopes immediately (Respond - Invalid Envelope, webhook path only).\n\nHTTP Request - Append to Event Store writes the raw event to an append-only log *before* any routing/delivery happens — this is what makes the bus replayable and auditable: even if every subscriber fails, the event itself is never lost."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "656a5c9d-5d58-403b-8823-7dbb8987558c",
      "name": "Sticky Note - Stage 2",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        1408,
        -336
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 6,
        "width": 1104,
        "height": 940,
        "content": "## 2. Routing & Fan-Out to Subscribers\n\nSwitch - Route by Event Type inspects `eventType` and sends the event down one or more of the matched branches, in parallel:\n- `order.created` → Subscriber - Order Service\n- `order.created` / `user.signed_up` → Subscriber - Notification Service (multiple types can share a subscriber)\n- any type → Subscriber - Analytics Service (catch-all / audit trail, always fires)\n- unmatched type → Code - Unknown Event Type (logged, not treated as an error)\n\nEach Subscriber node is an independent HTTP call with `continueOnFail` + `retryOnFail` set at the node level for transient-fault tolerance, but the workflow-level retry/backoff/DLQ logic (next stage) is what handles a subscriber that's fully down, not just flaky."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "1196e4ae-8693-4632-a7a3-74de5cbb55c7",
      "name": "Sticky Note - Stage 3",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        2544,
        -432
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 6,
        "width": 1200,
        "height": 1132,
        "content": "## 3. Retry, Dead-Letter Queue & Acknowledgement\n\nEach subscriber branch converges into a shared IF - Delivery Failed gate.\n\n- **Success** → HTTP Request - Ack Event Store (mark this event/subscriber pair as delivered) → done\n- **Failure** → Code - Compute Backoff & Retry Count checks how many attempts have been made (tracked in workflow static data, keyed by eventId+subscriber) against the configured max attempts.\n  - If attempts remain → Wait (exponential backoff) → loops back into the same subscriber call\n  - If attempts are exhausted → HTTP Request - Write to Dead Letter Queue persists the event + failure reason + attempt history, then Slack - DLQ Alert notifies the team a message needed manual intervention\n\nThis means a subscriber outage never loses events (they sit safely in the DLQ, replayable later) and never silently fails without anyone knowing."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
